<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Arne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1710">1710</a>–<a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Thomas Augustine Arne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Augustine Arne was an English composer of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> and early Classical periods and a leading British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> composer of the 18th century, working at Drury Lane and Covent Garden in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Born in Covent Garden, he wrote many operatic entertainments for the London theatres and pleasure gardens as well as concertos, sinfonias and sonatas. His song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a> was first heard in the Masque of Alfred at Cliveden, the country house of Frederick, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, in 1740. His Artaxerxes (1762) was one of the most successful English operas of the century and the only known attempt at an Italianate opera seria in English, with recitative in place of spoken dialogue.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-arne.jpg" alt="Thomas Arne" width="330" height="508" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Dunkarton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Arne.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Britain’s national symbols were made before the English word nationalism appeared, around 1798: John Arbuthnot invented John Bull in 1712, <b>Thomas Arne</b> composed “Rule, Britannia!” in 1740, and the Union Jack was adopted as the national flag in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…Charles Edward Stuart led the Jacobite rising of 1745, the song spread among supporters of King George II; it was sung at the London playhouses of Drury Lane and Covent Garden, <b>Thomas Arne</b> wrote a setting for Drury Lane, and The Gentleman's Magazine printed it in October 1745.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a></li><li><blockquote>The tune has likewise been credited to Wade, to John Reading and his son, to Handel, Gluck, Marcos Portugal, John IV and <b>Thomas Arne</b>, whom Wade knew.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-all-ye-faithful">O Come, All Ye Faithful</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Rule, Britannia!&quot; began as the final number of Alfred, a masque about Alfred the Great with music by <b>Thomas Arne</b> and words by James Thomson and David Mallet.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a></li></ul></section>
